the kerbside recycling collection scheme  that operates in team bridge now makes  it easier than ever to recycle more and  more from the comfort of your own home  most households in team bridge will be  given these containers for their waste  they'll have a green bin for composting  a black bin for landfill  and a black recycling box and a green  recycling box  however if you don't have room at your  property for all of these containers you  may be given sacks and if you live in a  communal property you'll be given these  bins to share  let's take a look at what can go into  your green recycling box  any glass bottle or jar so it could be  clear green or brown glass it doesn't  matter  we also take mixed plastic bottles so  that's any plastic bottle you use in the  household that could be  a fizzy drinks bottle  it could be a shampoo bottle it could  even be a detergent bottle as long as  it's a bottle and it's plastic that's  fine  however it might be a good idea to  actually squash your bottles before you  put them into your green box because we  have so many of them so remember once  you've washed it  however please remember that we can't  take all types of plastic in your  recycling box we can only take plastic  bottles  we don't want things like plastic food  trays or plastic food containers these  are all made out of so many different  grades of plastic it's really difficult  to recycle them  they have to go into your landfill bin  or if you ever visit the brunell road  recycling centre you can take them there  if you have any old mobile phones  printed cartridges or batteries like  these  you can pop them into a small plastic  bag  put them on your recycling box  and the crew will take them for you  so what can we put in the black  recycling box well any type of white  paper can go in there so that's going to  be your newspapers  magazines pieces of white office paper  directories catalogues and even white  window envelopes even ones with plastic  in you don't need to take that out  anymore that can stay in there  if you're not sure about what type of  paper you've got or whether it's colored  or whether it's white there's a simple  rule if it tears white it's all right so  for example if you get a magazine  tear a piece of the paper  if the fibers are white the little tiny  white hairy fibers there that means  that's fine to be recycled  what we don't want in the box is  anything like tissue paper or kitchen  roll because that's no good for  recycling that can go into a green  compost bin  textiles have now been added to the list  of things that can be recycled from home  so for example if you've got  old shirts jumpers  t-shirts  towels  blankets curtains even paired shoes  these can all be recycled from home all  you need to do  place them in a carrier bag  and tie it up  and leave it beside your recycling boxes  on your normal recycling collection day  however try not to use a black sack  because that could be mistaken for  ordinary refuse  can you make sure that all of your  textiles that you put out for collection  are clean and dry we don't want soiled  items  so as well as newspapers and magazines  you can also put old food and drink cans  in your black box as well  that could be anything like these old  food cans  it can even be  metal lids from your glass jars they can  go in as well so it's always a good idea  to pop them in your old cans maybe  squash them and then they won't fall out  when they're in your black box  it also includes aerosols as well  they're either made from steel or  aluminium so they can be recycled too  dirty food cans and jars can be smelly  so it's a good idea to keep your  recycling boxes clean by rinsing them  out using old dishwater it's also a lot  nicer for the collection cruise  if you sometimes forget what goes where  we have these free color box stickers  which are available from team bridge  recycling department and they'll help  you to remember  so let's take a look at what we can put  into the green composting bin  it's really important that we get this  bit right  firstly you can put any food waste in  there so it could be cooked food waste  uncooked food waste meat scraps plate  scrapings tea bags egg shells veggie  peelings  any food waste can go inside  all householders will have been given a  kitchen caddy to make it easier to put  all the compostable waste in the kitchen  it's a good idea to line your caddy with  a caddy liner so that your green bin  stays clean and smell free  remember that we only accept caddy  liners that are 100 compostable  and these should be light green with  dark green writing like this  brown paper caddy liners can also be  used  any other colored compostable bag will  not be accepted  this is so that the crew can easily tell  the difference between an approved liner  or a plastic bag  alternatively you can wrap your food  waste in newspaper  as well as food waste you can put garden  waste in your green bin too that can  include grass cuttings  hedge cuttings  as long as they're no more than seven  centimeters thick  but we don't want any soil in the  compost bin  we also accept thin cardboard in your  green composting bin  for example cereal packets  fruit packets  even egg boxes  but we don't want thick cardboard on  your green bin so say if you've had  something delivered like a big package  that would be thick cardboard  if you've got large amounts of thick  cardboard it's a good idea to take it to  your nearest recycling centre but if you  can't just put it into your black bin  please don't put anything in your green  bin that isn't compostable for example  textiles plastic bags and even these  cardboard drinks cartons you may think  they're just cardboard but they do  actually have a waxy lining inside that  wouldn't break down  before your green bin is emptied the  collection crew will look inside to make  sure that everything in there is  compostable  if they find something that isn't they  will 